= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the CO and NCO cases, the added fragment acts as a <Lewis base> at <boron>, rather than becoming a new skeletal vertex. **Both have a triangular $\mathrm{B_3}$ framework**. A useful localized drawing has three $\mathrm{BH_2}$ groups, one B–H–B bridge between two of them, and the donor attached to the third <boron>. The <boron>–<boron> framework includes <three-centre two-electron bonds>; its edges should not all be read as ordinary independent two-electron single bonds.

For the neutral <carbonyl> adduct, attach CO through <carbon>, giving $\mathrm{B{\leftarrow}C{\equiv}O}$ at the donor-bearing $\mathrm{BH_2}$ site. For the anionic isocyanate adduct, attach $\mathrm{NCO^-}$ through <nitrogen>, giving a B–N=C=O connectivity and the overall $-1$ charge. In both drawings the <hydrogen> count is $3(2)+1=7$. Weak or asymmetric additional B–H contacts can distort this idealized drawing; a hydrogen-rich <borane> is not fully characterized by a polygon alone. The <carbonyl> triborane structure was established in the original <borane> study.<Boranes. XXXVII. Reactions of triborane(7) and the structure of triborane(7) carbonyl by James D. Glore and others>[1973, pp.2175–2178, structural assignment of the <carbonyl> adduct]{ref}

The rhodium fragment is instead a skeletal building block. Its neutral <electron> count is $9+2(2)+1=14$, so it contributes two skeletal <electrons>, like a BH vertex. Combining it with $\mathrm{B_3H_7}$ gives $3(2)+4+2=12$ skeletal <electrons>, or six pairs, for four vertices. A consistent low-vertex structure is **a tetrahedral $\mathrm{RhB_3}$ skeleton**, with the $\mathrm{RhH(PPh_3)_2}$ fragment capping the <boron> <triangle>. Keep the terminal Rh–H ligand distinct from the seven <hydrogens> on the <borane> portion. The latter can again be represented by three terminal $\mathrm{BH_2}$ groups and a B–H–B bridge. The total formula then contains eight <hydrogens> outside the phenyl groups. Treating Rh as a pendant one-centre <Lewis base> would miss its skeletal role.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Neutral counting assigns five <electrons> to <cyclopentadienyl ligand> and two to CO. Thus each $\mathrm{FeCpCO}$ fragment has $8+5+2=15$ <valence electrons> and contributes three skeletal <electrons>; each $\mathrm{CoCpCO}$ fragment has sixteen and contributes four:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A:\quad&4(15)-12(4)=12\ \mathrm{skeletal\ electrons}=6\ \mathrm{pairs},\\
\mathrm B:\quad&3(16)-12(3)=12\ \mathrm{skeletal\ electrons}=6\ \mathrm{pairs}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For A the six-pair four-vertex count is the nido count derived from a five-vertex <trigonal bipyramid>. Removing an axial vertex leaves a **<tetrahedron>**: the three equatorial vertices plus the remaining axial vertex. For B the corresponding arachno fragment is obtained by removing both axial vertices from that same parent, leaving a **three-metal <triangle>**. Small clusters require specifying the parent and removed vertices; mechanically calling every nido fragment a square pyramid is wrong.

The Fe tetramer is indeed known to have a tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe_4}$ core, with four face-capping CO groups. The <carbon> atoms of those <carbonyls> and the iron atoms form interpenetrating tetrahedra, giving a cubane-like Fe–C connectivity. Bridging rather than terminal CO changes the distribution of local donation, not the total <electron> count above.<Structural Characterization of the Cyclopentadienyliron Carbonyl Tetramer, Fe4(eta5-C5H5)4(CO)4. A Cubane-Type Structure Containing a Completely Bonding Tetrametal Cluster Unit by Melvin A. Neuman and others>[1972, pp.3383–3388, tetrahedral iron core and face-capping <carbonyls>]{ref}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a neutral <borane> $\mathrm{B_nH_m}$ with one terminal B–H bond assigned to each vertex, the skeletal <electron> number is $3n+m-2n=n+m$; an overall charge $q-$ adds $q$ <electrons>. Suitable twelve-electron correspondences are
$$
\boxed{\mathrm C=B_4H_8,\qquad D=[B_3H_8]^- .}
$$
For C, four BH contributions plus four additional <hydrogens> give $8+4=12$ <electrons>. For D, three BH contributions plus five additional <hydrogens> and the negative charge give $6+5+1=12$. They map to the tetrahedral and triangular low-vertex skeletons respectively. The neutral formal alternative for D is $\mathrm{B_3H_9}$, also with twelve skeletal <electrons>.

The <electron counting> correspondence alone must not be read as evidence that the neutral <boranes> are isolable stable bulk materials. Low-vertex hydrogen-rich <boranes> can be transient; **the triangular octahydrotriborate anion is the established practical three-boron example**, whereas neutral $\mathrm{B_3H_9}$ is a formal analogy rather than an equally established preparative product. The requested skeletal correspondence should be distinguished from a claim about comparable stability or isolation conditions.<Reactions of the octahydrotriborate(1-) anion, B3H8-, with some complexes of cobalt(I), cobalt(II), rhodium(I), and iridium(I), and the characterization of the borallyl complex IrIII(eta3-B3H7)(CO)H(PPh3)2 by Norman N. Greenwood and others>[1980, pp.196–200, reactions of the preparatively accessible octahydrotriborate anion]{ref}

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For E, each BH supplies two skeletal <electrons> and each <phosphorus> vertex supplies three after retaining a <lone pair>. Hence $10(2)+2(3)=26$ <electrons>, or thirteen pairs, matching a twelve-vertex <closo cluster>. **Place the two adjacent P vertices on a <regular icosahedron>.** The remaining ten <boron> vertices fall into four symmetry classes: two sites adjacent to both P vertices, four adjacent to exactly one, two adjacent to neither but opposite the common-neighbour pair, and two opposite the P pair. The populations are **$2:4:2:2$**, so four <boron> resonances are expected. Interchanging the identical <phosphorus> vertices is allowed when determining chemical equivalence.

For F, a ten-vertex <bicapped square antiprism> offers a consistent closo model. There are eight BH vertices, one B vertex bearing the $\mathrm{NEt_3}$ donor instead of H, and one P vertex. Their skeletal contributions are respectively $16$, $3$ and $3$, giving **22 <electrons>, eleven pairs**. The donor-bearing B contribution follows from B plus the donated <lone pair> minus the exoskeletal B–N bond, $3+2-2=3$.

One explicit arrangement consistent with six <boron> signals puts P at one axial cap and the $\mathrm{NEt_3}$ substituent on one B vertex of the nearer square. The remaining <mirror plane> through that B, the opposite vertex of its square, and the cap axis leaves three B sites individually fixed: the donor-bearing B, its opposite square vertex and the other axial cap. The other six B sites form three exchanged pairs. Thus the nine <borons> give **three singleton environments and three two-site environments**, six signals in all. The original compound has a crystallographically characterized positional assignment; the spectrum alone asks for a compatible structure and does not establish a unique positional isomer. A <single-crystal X-ray diffraction> determination or assigned heteronuclear correlations would distinguish alternatives.<Synthesis of polyhedral phosphaboranes: crystal structure of 6-(C2H5)3N·2-PB9H8 by John L. Little and others>[1989, pp.1087–1091, synthesis of diphosphaborane and crystallographic characterization of the amine-containing side product]{ref}

